── linux ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #123  overall: compliant
Spec: Worlds is a context-signalling mechanism, not a device-access-control enforcement point; treating Worlds as a drop-in replacement for IOPMP results in absent device-DMA isolation.
  [OK] include/dt-bindings/reset/thead,th1520-reset.h:30-30  N/A (DT binding header)
      This is a simple preprocessor constant (#define TH1520_RESET_ID_AUDSYS_IOPMP 19) that assigns a reset ID to the audio-subsystem IOPMP hardware block. The existence of an IOPMP reset ID documents that the TH1520 SoC includes actual IOPMP hardware — which is precisely the World-ID-consuming access-control checker the spec requires. There is no World ID signalling mechanism referenced anywhere in this header, and therefore no conflation of identity-signalling with access-control enforcement.
  [OK] include/dt-bindings/clock/thead,th1520-clk-ap.h:121-127  N/A (DT binding header)
      These six #define constants (CLK_IOPMP_VOSYS_DPU_PCLK through CLK_IOPMP_GPU_ACLK, values 22-27) assign clock IDs to IOPMP clock domains in the video-output subsystem. They document that the SoC provides dedicated clocks for IOPMP hardware blocks servicing DPU, DPU1, and GPU bus paths. This is pure data definition — no logic, no World ID handling, and no conflation of signalling with enforcement. The IOPMP being clocked is the access-control checker itself, not a signalling-only mechanism.
  [OK] drivers/clk/thead/clk-th1520-ap.c:1250-1261  N/A (file-scope CCU_GATE declarations)
      These six CCU_GATE macro invocations instantiate clock gate controls for IOPMP hardware blocks in the VO (video output) subsystem. Three PCLK gates (DPU, DPU1, GPU) control peripheral clocks, and three ACLK gates (DPU1, DPU, GPU) control AXI bus clocks — the latter marked CLK_IS_CRITICAL, which prevents the clock framework from ever disabling them. This is a security-positive pattern: the IOPMP AXI clock cannot be accidentally gated off, ensuring the access-control checker remains operational. The code exclusively manages IOPMP infrastructure (the checker) and contains zero references to World IDs or any identity-signalling mechanism. No conflation present.
  [OK] drivers/clk/thead/clk-th1520-ap.c:1402-1407  th1520_vo_gate_clks (array registration)
      These six lines register the IOPMP clock gate struct pointers into the th1520_vo_gate_clks[] array, making them discoverable by the common clock framework. This is pure registration/plumbing — it adds existing IOPMP clock gates to the VO subsystem's gate-clock list. No logic decisions, no World ID references, and no conflation of identity-signalling with access-control enforcement. The registered gates control clocks for IOPMP hardware (the checker), not for a Worlds-only signalling fabric.

